---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import FactList from '../../components/FactList.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/Diagram.astro';
import RcArchitecture from '../../components/diagrams/RcArchitecture.astro';
import RcCrashTimeline from '../../components/diagrams/RcCrashTimeline.astro';

const toc: [string, string][] = [
  ['problem', 'The problem'],
  ['built', 'What I built'],
  ['architecture', 'Architecture'],
  ['challenges', 'Hardest challenges'],
  ['evidence', 'Evidence'],
  ['limits', 'Tradeoffs and limits'],
  ['next', 'What I would improve'],
  ['links', 'Links'],
];
---
<CaseStudy
  title="RyuCloud: a crash-safe video pipeline case study | Joshua Abrogena"
  description="A Go and Next.js video pipeline with direct-to-storage uploads, lease-based crash recovery and CloudFront signed cookies, deployed once on AWS with Terraform."
  kind="Cloud video pipeline"
  name="RyuCloud"
  status="Locally complete. Deployed to AWS once on 2026-11-14 (owner-reported; no raw output or screenshots were kept), then destroyed. Not production-ready. ryucloud.link is currently down, so there is no live demo."
  stack="Go, PostgreSQL 17, FFmpeg, Redis and SQS, Next.js 15, Terraform, AWS (ECS Fargate, RDS, S3, CloudFront)"
  meta={[
    ['Kind', 'Solo portfolio project. No users, no revenue.'],
    ['Written with', 'AI assistants (Claude Code) helped write and review code and docs; the About page explains how I work.'],
  ]}
  toc={toc}
>
  <Section n={1} id="problem" title="The problem">
    <p>A video platform has two awkward properties. The files are large, so they should never pass through the API. And transcoding is slow and fails in interesting ways: a worker is killed halfway, a message is delivered twice, the database blips as a job finishes, two workers race for the same job. A single upload still has to end up as exactly one playable video.</p>
    <p>I built the core of a YouTube-style platform to work through that: accounts, channels, direct uploads, an asynchronous HLS pipeline and private, authorised playback.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section n={2} id="built" title="What I built">
    <p>What exists, and what does not.</p>
    <div class="twocol">
      <div><h3>Built and tested locally</h3>
<ul>
                <li>Accounts and sessions (Argon2id, hashed session tokens, CSRF origin check, login throttling), channels, visibility rules</li>
                <li>Uploads straight to private object storage with a presigned URL whose size and type are signed in</li>
                <li>A separate Go worker: FFprobe and FFmpeg produce 360p and 720p HLS and a thumbnail, with leases, retries and a sweeper</li>
                <li>Next.js studio and watch pages with an hls.js player; a Docker Compose stack with MinIO and Redis</li>
                <li>A Terraform stack for AWS, applied once; CI with five jobs</li>
              </ul></div>
      <div><h3>Not built, or never exercised</h3>
<ul>
                <li>The manual deploy workflow: written, never run</li>
                <li>Search, comments, subscriptions, view counts (a search box exists as a placeholder)</li>
                <li>Resumable or multipart uploads, subtitles, DRM</li>
                <li>Autoscaling, multi-AZ, WAF, backup restore: planned only</li>
                <li>Production operation of any kind</li>
              </ul></div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section n={3} id="architecture" title="System architecture">
    <Diagram id="rc-arch" label="System architecture on AWS" description="The browser reaches a load balancer that serves a Next.js app and a Go API. The browser uploads video directly to a private S3 bucket using a presigned URL. The API records the upload in PostgreSQL and puts a job ID on an SQS queue. A Go worker with FFmpeg takes the job, claims it in PostgreSQL, reads the source from S3 and writes HLS files back. Playback goes through CloudFront with signed cookies.">
      <RcArchitecture />
    </Diagram>
    <p>The API is a modular monolith in Go with hand-written SQL and a hand-maintained OpenAPI file. The worker is a second binary sharing the same packages. An upload works like this: the API validates the request and returns a presigned PUT; the browser sends the file straight to the bucket; the API then checks the stored object's size and type against what was declared, and in one transaction marks the video queued and inserts the job row. Only then does it publish the job ID to the queue, on a best-effort basis, because the row is already durable.</p>

    <h3>Decisions and what each cost</h3>
    <div class="tablewrap" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Table: From the project's documented tradeoffs">
      <table>
        <caption>From the project's documented tradeoffs.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Decision</th><th scope="col">Choice</th><th scope="col">Tradeoff accepted</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Job state</th><td>PostgreSQL is the source of truth; the queue is a hint</td><td>Simple and tolerant of duplicates. Crash recovery latency equals the lease, not instant.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Sessions</th><td>Server-side sessions, not JWTs</td><td>Immediate revocation and one indexed lookup per request.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Status updates</th><td>Polling every 3 s, not WebSockets</td><td>Less moving machinery; slightly slower to update.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Playback</th><td>CloudFront signed cookies on a sibling domain, with a signer written against the standard library</td><td>No new dependency to pin, but about 100 lines to maintain; cookies cannot be revoked before they expire; needs a domain you control.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Cost</th><td>No NAT, public task IPs, single-AZ database, Spot worker</td><td>Cheaper, with weaker network isolation and less resilience.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section n={4} id="challenges" title="The three hardest problems">
    <h3>1. A background pipeline that stays correct under crashes, duplicates and retries</h3>
    <p>The queue (Redis locally, SQS on AWS) delivers at least once and is not a store of truth, so it carries only a job ID. The truth lives in a <code>processing_jobs</code> row with database constraints, and the design follows from that:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Claim</strong> is one conditional <code>UPDATE</code>. With N workers racing, exactly one matches and the others drop the message.</li>
      <li><strong>Lease and heartbeat.</strong> Every change to the job requires that this worker still owns it; otherwise the worker abandons its work.</li>
      <li><strong>Sweeper.</strong> Every worker periodically re-sends jobs that have no live owner, using <code>FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Acknowledge only after commit.</strong> The result transaction (job succeeded, video ready, rendition rows) commits first; the message is acknowledged afterwards.</li>
      <li><strong>Idempotent outputs.</strong> Object keys depend only on the video ID, leftovers are deleted before upload, and the master playlist is uploaded last.</li>
    </ul>
    <Diagram id="rc-crash" label="Crash recovery" description="Worker A claims a job, which sets attempts to 1 and a 120 second lease, and starts encoding. It is killed at about 2 percent progress. The lease runs out. A sweeper notices the job has no live owner and puts the job ID back on the queue. Worker B claims it with attempts equal to 2, finishes, commits the result and only then acknowledges the message.">
      <RcCrashTimeline />
    </Diagram>
    <p>Tradeoffs: recovery takes up to about two minutes (the lease), a retry restarts the encode from the beginning, and progress visibly resets.</p>

    <h3>2. A CloudFront signed-cookie bug that a unit test could not see</h3>
    <p>The bucket is fully private and video reaches viewers only through CloudFront, using three signed cookies that the API sets after its normal visibility checks. An HLS playlist refers to its segments by relative path, which is why cookies are used instead of a signature in the URL.</p>
    <p>On the first cloud test, uploads and processing worked, but playback failed: the playlist and segments returned 403 while a signed thumbnail returned 200. Decoding the cookie policy showed its resource ended in <code>%2A</code> rather than <code>*</code>. The code had built the URL with Go's URL type, which percent-encodes the wildcard, and CloudFront matches the literal text. The unit test had only signed a hand-written resource string, so it never exercised the real code path.</p>
<pre tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Code comparison: broken and fixed policy resource"><code>Resource in the cookie policy (from the project's verification log)
  broken:  https://media.&lt;domain&gt;/videos/&lt;id&gt;/%2A
  fixed:   built as  "https://" + domain + "/" + VideoPrefix + "*"</code></pre>
    <p>The fix builds the string directly, and a new test asserts that the real grant path keeps the literal wildcard. Only the API image had to be rebuilt. The lesson is to test what the handler builds, not what the helper accepts.</p>

    <h3>3. Deploying and tearing down within fifteen-minute credentials</h3>
    <p>I used short-lived keyless credentials (a login with MFA, no access keys) that last about fifteen minutes, to apply a 115-resource plan and then prove it could be destroyed. Two applies failed mid-run with expired tokens, leaving a lock and an error state file. Recovery: read the lock ID from the lock object, force-unlock, push the saved error state back, and untaint the database, which was healthy in AWS and would otherwise have been replaced, then apply in stages.</p>
    <p>Before that, I had to correct my own earlier claim. Registering the domain had created a second hosted zone, and the registry pointed at it, so certificates could never validate. A check I had called "delegation verified" had used my local resolver instead of the registry. The fix was to repoint the name servers. The lesson that a single apply must fit inside the credential window is not yet written into the runbook.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section n={5} id="evidence" title="Evidence">
    <p>Two groups, with different trust levels. The local results from 2026-11-10 were run in an assistant session where the output was seen (<strong>Run</strong>). The AWS session on 2026-11-14 is <strong>User-reported</strong>: <strong>no raw output or screenshots were kept</strong>, so it cannot be independently checked. This project has no screenshots and no recording at all, so the evidence here is text and code. I ran none of these checks again while writing this page.</p>

    <h3>Local tests and checks</h3>
    <FactList ids={['rcCrash', 'rcWorkerTests', 'rcE2e', 'rcVulns']} />
    <p>The test inventory counted from source is 232 Go test functions across 9 packages. The frontend suite is reported as 106 passing tests; a text search finds only 79 test calls, presumably because some are parametrised, so I quote the reported figure with that caveat.</p>

    <h3>The AWS session</h3>
    <FactList ids={['rcCloud', 'rcBug', 'rcCfChecks', 'rcJobs']} />
    <p>After the session, an audit script checked each service for leftovers directly (ECS, load balancers, NAT gateways, addresses, databases, buckets, queues, registries, secrets, CloudFront, certificates, logs, IAM) and reported none. Its tag-based search listed 19 stale entries, each of which a direct check then showed to be gone; that is a flaw in the script, not yet fixed.</p>

    <h3>Not done</h3>
    <FactList ids={['rcNotRun']} />
  </Section>

  <Section n={6} id="limits" title="Tradeoffs and limits">
    <ul>
      <li>No latency, throughput, concurrency, transcode-speed, storage or cost figures exist. Cost estimates in the project's notes are list-price calculations, not measurements, and are not repeated here.</li>
      <li>The AWS evidence is owner-recorded and unrecorded: counts such as 115 planned, 22 added and 94 destroyed are not reconciled in the repository.</li>
      <li>Tasks have public IPs and no NAT, there is no WAF, no backups were restored, and the database connection does not verify the server certificate.</li>
      <li>The login throttle is in memory, so it is per process, not shared across tasks. Registration discloses whether an email exists. There is no email verification or password reset.</li>
      <li>Uploads are single-part only, encodes are CPU-only and sequential, and output is MPEG-TS HLS only.</li>
      <li>Two npm audit findings (one high, one moderate) were accepted as build-time only; the fix needs a major framework upgrade.</li>
      <li>Some of the project's own documents still describe the pre-deployment state and are out of date.</li>
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section n={7} id="next" title="What I would improve next">
    <p>These come from the project's own list and are planned, not done.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Repeat the AWS session capturing logs, screenshots and cost data as it happens, so the claims have proof attached.</li>
      <li>Run failure drills on AWS (kill tasks, fill the dead-letter queue), a database restore, and a cookie-expiry and Safari playback check.</li>
      <li>Add a test that goes through the real playback-grant path and parses the policy it produces, which would have caught the wildcard bug before AWS.</li>
      <li>Run the deploy workflow end to end, and write staged applies into the runbook.</li>
      <li>Move to private subnets or endpoints, verify the database certificate, and add multipart uploads.</li>
    </ol>
  </Section>

  <Section n={8} id="links" title="Links">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Repository:</strong> <a href="https://github.com/jabrogena5100/RyuCloud" rel="noopener noreferrer">github.com/jabrogena5100/RyuCloud</a> (public). Some of its own documents still describe the pre-deployment state; this page is the current account.</li>
      <li><strong>Live demo:</strong> none. ryucloud.link is currently down; the AWS stack was destroyed after testing.</li>
      <li><a href="/projects/">All projects</a></li>
    </ul>
  </Section>
</CaseStudy>
